    {
      "ts": 1790156363.458509,
      "status": "done",
      "result": {
        "summary": "Reviewed orchestrator/overhead.py, cli.py wiring, and both test files against the packet's acceptance criteria. for_goal correctly splits orchestration/execution roles, computes amplification/cost_share/latency_share with proper None-handling, and reuses scorecard._planner_usage_totals/_planner_runs_for_goal as required (verified against scorecard.py's own by_goal precedent -- fix-round tasks are created with role='execute' and parent inherited from the original goal, so flat goal_id filtering is correct and matches how by_goal itself aggregates). report() builds totals/medians correctly, including the degenerate case where unattributed_tokens is identical across all goal rows. CLI wiring (--overhead flag, mutual exclusivity, --goal/--json handling) mirrors existing flag patterns correctly. All three test_overhead.py cases and the test_cli.py case trace through correctly against the implementation. One low-severity robustness concern noted on the hardcoded parents[3] path in test_report_over_real_run_rows; nothing blocking.",
        "verdict": "approve",
        "comments": [
          {
            "path": "tests/test_overhead.py",
            "line": 45,
            "issue": "test_report_over_real_run_rows finds the real .orchestrator dir via Path(__file__).resolve().parents[3], hardcoding a fixed wt/<task-id> nesting depth instead of following the repo's own parents[1] convention (tests/_harness.py). Verified this worktree's own .orchestrator/runs is empty (gitignored) while the main checkout two levels up has real data, so the choice is deliberate and currently passes, but it's fragile: run from a different depth or the main checkout directly and this acceptance test fails for reasons unrelated to the code.",
            "severity": "low"
          }
        ],
        "packet_version": "6d76047d2ef8",
